---
import Entry from '../../layouts/Entry.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';
import Specimen from '../../components/Specimen.astro';
---
<Entry slug="usaa-closure-reasons">
  <h2><span class="label">The question</span>Why are members closing their cards?</h2>
  <p>
    Retention teams can only make a good offer if they know why someone is leaving. When I pulled a year of
    credit-card closure records in Snowflake, <mark>72% of closures</mark> were logged under a single code,
    <em>“No longer needed.”</em> More than 25,000 closures carried essentially no reason at all.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="label">What I did</span>Let the phone calls say what the dropdown couldn’t</h2>
  <Note>The dropdown wasn’t wrong, just too coarse. Reps picked the closest option because the real one didn’t exist.</Note>
  <ul>
    <li>Found and sized the gap in SQL, then traced it to the closure-reason dropdown reps use during the call.</li>
    <li>Embedded the unstructured call transcripts and clustered them with <strong>BERTopic</strong> in Python.</li>
    <li>Labeled the clusters into reasons the code was hiding, such as financial hardship, a competitor’s rate offer, and fraud-related closures.</li>
  </ul>

  <Specimen rows={[
    ['Closures under one vague code', '72%'],
    ['Reason capture rate', '28% → 66%'],
    ['Closures without a reason', '25,000+'],
    ['Recommendations to leadership', '3'],
  ]} />

  <h2><span class="label">What came of it</span>Three recommendations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Retrain reps</strong> on accurate reason capture.</li>
    <li><strong>Redesign the dropdown</strong>: declutter it and add the missing reasons the clusters surfaced.</li>
    <li><strong>Predict the reason before the call starts</strong>, so the rep opens with the right retention offer.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="mono" style="color: var(--pencil); font-size: 0.8rem;">
    Internal work. No company data, code, or transcripts are shown here.
  </p>
</Entry>
